    "body": "Insmed will bolster Sanofi’s immunology and inflammation portfolio, which is currently led by Regeneron-partnered blockbuster Dupixent.\nSanofi’s new CEO Belen Garijo certainly knows how to make a first impression. Just shy of seven full months in the role, Garijo has struck biopharma’s largest M&A deal of the year, with the French pharma offering $41 billion in cash for rare disease and immunology company Insmed.\nInsmed will bolster Sanofi’s immunology and inflammation portfolio, which is currently led by Regeneron-partnered blockbuster Dupixent.\nStars of the acquisition are Insmed’s approved products Brinsupri for non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis (NCFB) and Arikayce, an inhaled therapy for mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) lung disease. Insmed also has a deep pipeline of therapies in respiratory disease, I&I, neurological disease and rare disease.\nMergers & acquisitions\nBig Pharma is ready to strike, with J&J most likely to buy: Leerink\nThe largest pharmas have a collective $188 billion in firepower to spend on M&A transactions, with all but one signaling a desire to execute, according to a new analysis from Leerink Partners.\nOctober 28, 2026\n·\n3 min read\n·\nAnnalee Armstrong\nRead more\nInsmed’s shares spiked over 14% to $170.79 in pre-market trading on Tuesday morning after the deal was announced. Sanofi also rose more than 5% to $49.29.\nSanofi offered $175 per share for Insmed, with the deal expected to close in the first half of 2027.\nLeaders from Sanofi will hold a conference call at 8 am ET.\nThis story will be updated.",
